<h2>Mining software should connect authorized rights to measured operations and accountable payments</h2><p>A mining or extractives platform can connect licences, contracts, sites, exploration, work programmes, production, stockpiles, quality, transport, sales, assets, procurement, costs and payments. The essential control is traceability: each reported quantity or financial obligation should lead back to approved source evidence.</p><p>Government authorities, licence holders and qualified geological, mining, environmental, safety, legal, finance and community professionals remain responsible for rights, reserves, designs, permits, measurements, hazards, disclosures and payments. Software does not grant a licence, certify a reserve, authorize blasting or declare a site safe or compliant.</p>
<h2>Licences, contracts, areas and obligations</h2><ul><li>Licence or contract identifier, holder, area, commodity and status</li><li>Issue, effective, renewal, relinquishment and expiry dates</li><li>Work, reporting, payment, environmental and social obligations</li><li>Approved maps, coordinates, documents and amendments</li><li>Responsible owners, due dates, evidence and review status</li><li>Restricted and publicly disclosable information as authorized</li></ul><p>The authoritative legal record remains with the responsible authority. The institutional system should reference verified terms and preserve amendment history rather than recreate legal rights from assumptions.</p>
<h2>Exploration, samples and technical information</h2><p>Exploration records may connect programmes, locations, surveys, drilling, samples, laboratories, results, documents and costs. Sample identity and chain of custody need stable references and correction history. Resource or reserve estimates, classifications and technical interpretations require qualified professional methods and approval outside ordinary software automation.</p>
<h2>Work plans, shifts, crews and contractors</h2><p>Approved work can be organized by site, pit, underground area, bench, activity, shift, crew, equipment and contractor. Plans may connect target quantities, access, dependencies, permits, inspections and completion evidence. Scheduling must respect safety and technical authority; a production target cannot override a stop-work decision.</p>
<h2>Production measurement and stockpiles</h2><p>Production events should retain source location, material, quantity, unit, time, measurement method, equipment, operator and approval. Movements can connect extraction, hauling, processing, recovery, waste, stockpiles and dispatch. Survey, scale, assay and reconciliation differences need visible review rather than silent adjustment.</p>
<h2>Quality, sampling and chain of custody</h2><p>Material quality may be supported by sampling plans, sample identifiers, collection, transport, laboratory results, review and approved grade or specification. The system can preserve links and status but cannot determine sampling adequacy or technical acceptance without qualified owners.</p>
<h2>Transport, weighbridge, sales and delivery</h2><p>Dispatch can connect vehicle or carrier, driver, source stockpile, destination, gross and tare weights, net quantity, documents, seals, checkpoints and proof of delivery. Sales or offtake workflows may connect contracts, price references, quality adjustments, invoices, collections and settlement. Measurement and valuation rules require authorization.</p>
<h2>Equipment, maintenance, fuel and critical inventory</h2><p>Assets can be organized by site and functional location with meters, inspections, preventive work, failures, downtime, parts and cost. Fuel, tyres, spare parts and other critical stock need controlled receipt, issue and reconciliation. Explosives or other hazardous materials require separate legal, security and professional controls; generic inventory rules are not sufficient.</p>
<h2>Environment, health, safety and community evidence</h2><p>The platform may record approved inspections, incidents, actions, training, monitoring points, permits, grievances, commitments and rehabilitation activities. Qualified owners define hazards, thresholds, investigation, medical, environmental and community responses. A dashboard is not a substitute for field verification or professional judgment.</p>
<h2>Royalties, taxes, fees and other payments</h2><p>Payment obligations may depend on licence terms, production, sales, price, area, period or other approved bases. The system can connect calculations, declarations, invoices, payments, receipts and reconciliation. Legal and finance owners approve the obligation, formula, currency, due date and disclosure level.</p>
<h2>Cost, budget and project control</h2><p>Budgets and actual costs may be analyzed by licence, site, phase, work programme, pit, process, equipment, contractor or cost centre. Procurement commitments, inventory issues, payroll inputs, services and capital projects can connect to finance. Reports should distinguish operational quantities from accounting estimates.</p>
<h2>Remote operation, security and continuity</h2><p>Sites with limited connectivity may need controlled offline capture, queued synchronization, device identity, conflict handling and later reconciliation. Access should separate legal, technical, production, warehouse, finance, EHS and administration roles. Sensitive coordinates, commercial terms and personal information require restricted handling.</p>
<h2>Implementation, migration and UAT</h2><p>Implementation maps commodities, licence structures, locations, units, measurements, workflows, roles, reports and integrations. Migration should prioritize active rights, obligations, approved masters, opening stockpiles, assets, balances and open work. UAT should cover measurement correction, stockpile reconciliation, rejected quality, failed dispatch, payment adjustments, stop-work controls and offline recovery.</p>
<h2>Governance references</h2><p>The data-accountability model was cross-checked against the <a href="https://eiti.org/eiti-requirements">2023 EITI Requirements</a> for licences, production and payment or revenue information, and the IFC’s <a href="https://www.ifc.org/en/insights-reports/general-environmental-health-and-safety-guidelines/ehs-guidelines-oil-gas-mining">Mining EHS Guidelines</a>. Applicability depends on the organization, project and jurisdiction; these references are not certification or legal advice.</p>
