    private function agriculture(): array
    {
        return $this->page(
            slug: 'agriculture-agribusiness-software-afghanistan',
            title: ['en' => 'Agriculture & Agribusiness Software in Afghanistan', 'fa' => 'نرم‌افزار زراعت و تجارت زراعتی در افغانستان', 'ps' => 'په افغانستان کې د کرنې او کرنیز کاروبار سافټویر'],
            eyebrow: ['en' => 'Traceable farms, seasons, inputs, harvest lots, storage and markets', 'fa' => 'farm، season، input، harvest lot، storage و market قابل پیگیری', 'ps' => 'د تعقیب وړ farm، season، input، harvest lot، storage او market'],
            summary: ['en' => 'Connect producers, farms and plots, seasonal plans, inputs, field work, harvest lots, aggregation, quality, storage, processing, sales, logistics, costs and settlements.', 'fa' => 'producer، farm/plot، seasonal plan، input، field work، harvest lot، aggregation، quality، storage، processing، sale، logistics، cost و settlement را وصل کنید.', 'ps' => 'producer، farm/plot، seasonal plan، input، field work، harvest lot، aggregation، quality، storage، processing، sale، logistics، cost او settlement ونښلوئ.'],
            body: [
                'en' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Agriculture software should preserve the chain from farm activity to sale</h2><p>An agriculture or agribusiness platform can connect producers, farms and plots, seasons, crops, inputs, field activities, harvest lots, aggregation, grading, storage, processing, contracts, sales, delivery, costs and settlements. Stable producer, location, season and lot identities make traceability useful.</p><p>Software records are not legal proof of land ownership or use rights. Qualified agronomy, veterinary, food-safety, quality, environmental, legal and commercial owners must define advice, treatments, withdrawal periods, certifications, grading and acceptance. The application records approved decisions; it does not make them professionally.</p>
<h2>Producer, farm, plot and season records</h2><ul><li>Producer, household, cooperative, group or commercial-farm references</li><li>Farm and plot identifiers, location and authorized boundaries</li><li>Season, crop or enterprise, variety and intended market</li><li>Responsible field team, agreement and consent status</li><li>Documents, visits and approved eligibility information</li><li>Privacy, sharing and retention rules for personal or geospatial data</li></ul><p>Location and participant records should distinguish reported, mapped and verified information. They must not be presented as cadastral evidence or a land-right determination.</p>
<h2>Seasonal planning and approved production protocols</h2><p>Plans may connect crop, plot, dates, target area, activities, inputs, labour, equipment, irrigation, budget and expected output. Agronomic recommendations and changes require the organization’s qualified owners and local evidence. A schedule or alert cannot determine what is safe or suitable for a farm.</p>
<h2>Inputs, procurement, storage and controlled issue</h2><p>Seed, fertilizer, crop-protection products, feed, packaging and other inputs can connect approved catalogue data, supplier, purchase, batch or lot, expiry where applicable, storage, issue, recipient, plot and balance. Restricted or hazardous inputs require applicable legal, safety and professional controls.</p>
<h2>Field activities, observations and mobile capture</h2><p>Field teams may record visits, land preparation, planting, irrigation, input application, labour, machinery, observations, photos and follow-up. Offline capture needs device and user identity, timestamps, queued synchronization, conflict handling and review. Location or image metadata should be collected only for a defined and authorized purpose.</p>
<h2>Livestock and animal records where required</h2><p>Livestock workflows may connect animal or herd identity, location, feed, production, movement and approved health events. Diagnosis, treatment, medicine, withdrawal and welfare decisions belong to qualified veterinary and animal-health professionals. Access to sensitive producer and animal information should be controlled.</p>
<h2>Harvest lots, weights, grading and acceptance</h2><p>Harvest should create a lot or batch linked to producer, plot, season, crop, date, quantity, unit and collection event. Weights, moisture, grade, quality test, rejection, price basis and authorized acceptance can remain attached. Corrections require history rather than replacement of the original evidence.</p>
<h2>Aggregation, cooperatives and contract farming</h2><p>Collection centres and cooperatives may aggregate multiple producer lots while preserving their source relationships. Contract-farming records can connect agreement, commitments, input advances, delivery, quality, pricing and settlement. The responsible legal and commercial teams approve contract meaning and enforcement.</p>
<h2>Storage, cold chain, condition and loss</h2><p>Warehouse or cold-room records can preserve location, lot, quantity, condition, storage requirements, inspections, movements and losses. Temperature or humidity readings may support review but do not by themselves certify product safety. Qualified owners determine disposition and release.</p>
<h2>Processing, packaging and manufacturing links</h2><p>Cleaning, sorting, drying, milling, packing or other transformation can connect input lots to output lots, yields, by-products, quality and cost. The processing model should preserve traceability through splits, combinations, rework and packaging without claiming unsupported equivalence.</p>
<h2>Sales, contracts, logistics and delivery</h2><p>Sales may connect customer, product or grade, lot allocation, quantity, price, contract, order, dispatch, vehicle or carrier, proof of delivery, invoice and collection. Export, inspection, certificate or customs requirements must be confirmed by authorized owners for the specific commodity and market.</p>
<h2>Cost, finance and producer settlement</h2><p>Costs can be analyzed by farm, plot, season, crop, activity, lot, facility or contract. Input advances, purchases, fees, deductions, bonuses and payments can support a transparent producer or partner statement. Accounting and commercial teams approve valuation, allocation, currency and settlement rules.</p>
<h2>Traceability, quality events and recall readiness</h2><p>The system should answer where a lot came from, what approved activities and inputs were recorded, how it was aggregated or transformed, where it is stored and where it was delivered. Complaint, hold, withdrawal or recall workflows should preserve scope, decision, communication, recovered quantities and closure evidence.</p>
<h2>Analytics, weather and remote-sensing boundaries</h2><p>Dashboards may combine operational, financial, weather or remote-sensing data, but source, date, resolution, uncertainty and missing coverage must be visible. A satellite or model output is an inference, not field verification, legal proof or agronomic instruction.</p>
<h2>Implementation, migration and UAT</h2><p>Implementation maps producers, locations, seasons, crops, inputs, activities, lots, quality, facilities, commercial rules, roles and integrations. Migration should validate duplicates, consent, location references, active seasons, opening stock, contracts, advances and balances. UAT should cover offline capture, lot split and merge, rejected quality, storage loss, partial delivery, settlement correction and traceability search.</p>
<h2>Digital agriculture reference</h2><p>The traceability and evidence model was cross-checked against FAO resources on <a href="https://sti-portal.fao.org/families/digital-certification-and-traceability-platforms">digital certification and traceability platforms</a> and <a href="https://openknowledge.fao.org/server/api/core/bitstreams/939c996b-f10b-49ef-993c-3e5e6e0f1478/content">digital agriculture and responsible data use</a>. These references do not confer certification or replace local professional and legal review.</p>
